17592186045417] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429515 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429516 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429517 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1031 "Rates of Change in Applied Contexts" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1033 620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1034 1.7808196977383024 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049658 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "08d376d5-c4e1-5517-9c3d-afeaa9f44009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050234 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050234 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050234 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating Related Rates Using the Pythagorean Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050234 #edb/ref 1033 345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050234 #edb/ref 1034 1.754968118335739 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050234 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "38146cba-4e2a-55b0-9106-e7c8e4e7b732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064026 #edb/ref 1069 "e-228" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064026 #edb/ref 1071 "A  $15\\,\\textrm{ft}$ ladder is leaning against a wall. If the top of the ladder is sliding down at a rate of $1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s},$ how fast is the bottom of the ladder moving away from the wall when the top of the ladder is $9\\,\\textrm{ft}$ from the ground?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064026 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b11d3606-7700-5c0f-89da-26b22a0ffd9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064026 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/9e/9e0fc4dc0e9ed182c4bfdcd3b127f9343fa6030be8c549c77880c6998a265c8b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the bottom of the ladder,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the ladder.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ \n\nWe know that $y = 9\\,\\textrm{ft}$ and $z=15\\,\\textrm{ft}.$ We can find $x$ using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{9}^{2} & ={15}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =225-81 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =144 \\\\ x & =12\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = -1\\,\\textrm{ft}/\\textrm{s},$\n\n- Note that the length of the ladder remains the same, so $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(9)(-1) & =2(15)(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{18}{24} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ladder is sliding away at a rate of $0.75\\text{ ft}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064027 #edb/ref 1069 "e-229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064027 #edb/ref 1071 "A rocket is launched vertically and is being tracked by a radar station which is located on the ground $8\\,\\textrm{km}$ from the launch site. If the speed of the rocket is $1700\\,\\textrm{km}/\\textrm{h}$ when it is $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ from the ground, how fast is the distance between the radar station and the rocket increasing?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0284ca7f-ee39-5b9f-87f7-df109aa2200d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064027 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/8c/8c28dc5302e1f6e8e6ed905737b1487b7a2c6fcea7603c75eecca037ab64ebd1.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the radar station and the launch site,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the rocket and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the radar station and the rocket.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nWe know that $y = 15\\,\\textrm{km}$ and $x=8\\,\\textrm{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {8}^{2}+{15}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 64+225 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 289 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =17\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = 1700\\,\\textrm{km}/\\textrm{h}$ when $y=15,$\n\n- the distance between the radar station and the launch site ($x$) remains the same, so $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(8)(0)+2(15)(1700) & =2(17)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 51000 & =34\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =1500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the radar station and the rocket is increasing at a rate of $1500\\,\\textrm{km}/\\textrm{h}$ when the rocket is $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ from the ground." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064382 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064383 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064436 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064436 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064436 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064436 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064436 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Related Rates Using the Pythagorean Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064436 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064436 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064436 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064436 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186064436 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f68a8ca8-1207-5a7d-8384-c71245f6f467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065321 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065321 #edb/ref 1061 915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065321 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd33c842-935a-5ebb-af04-0637b5fac369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065321 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065322 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065322 #edb/ref 1061 916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065322 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d5ac3b9-0ce8-515d-a75f-8dfe5e48a2ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065322 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065323 #edb/ref 1061 920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065323 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d0abf134-e37f-5d32-934e-6bd0afc76e75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065323 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065385 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we have a $10\\,\\textrm{ft}$ ladder that is leaning against the wall. A man is pushing the bottom of the ladder towards the wall at a rate of $1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s}.$ How fast is the top of the ladder sliding up the wall when the bottom is at a distance of $8\\,\\textrm{ft}$ from the wall?\n\nTo solve this and similar problems, we can follow the four steps below.\n\n**Step 1:** Draw a diagram. \n\n![](images/42/42fce00bf688c28024f568b75e60335e40e81dbc266abf64adde28efe235a9f3.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the bottom of the ladder and the wall,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the ladder.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle.\n\n** Step 2:** Use the Pythagorean theorem to find the missing side.\n\nAt the particular moment in question we have $x = 8\\textrm{ ft}$ and $z=10\\textrm{ ft}.$ So, we can find $y$ at this moment using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {8}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={10}^{2} \\\\ 64+{y}^{2} & =100 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n** Step 3:** Identify the variables that change with time.\n\n- The distance of the ladder from the wall ($x$) changes with time at a rate of $1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s},$ which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = -1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s}.\n$$\n\nThe rate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ is the velocity of the bottom of the ladder. It is negative because the ladder moves towards the wall (so $x$ becomes smaller with time).\n\n- The length of the ladder ($z$) is fixed and doesn't change with time, so\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 0.\n$$\n\n- The distance of the top of the ladder from the ground ($y$) changes with time, and it's what we are looking for:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = \\textrm{?}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Take the derivative with respect to time and plug in the known values.\n\nTo find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t},$ we differentiate both sides of $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with respect to $t,$ using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}-2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values and solve for the unknown rate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(6)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(10)(0)-2(8)(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{0+16}{12} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the ladder slides up the wall at a rate of approx $1.33 \\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s}$ when the bottom is at a distance of $8\\,\\textrm{ft}$ from the wall." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065385 #edb/ref 1065 492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065385 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "519a6401-a713-563f-bb3e-49181cd7bc50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065385 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235944 #edb/ref 1080 "0.7\\,\\text{km/h}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235944 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235944 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "152f09c1-2b4c-5050-bb9c-533f0063cd43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235977 #edb/ref 1080 "0.8\\,\\text{km/h}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235977 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235977 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "197976b6-d8c4-5eaa-a2b0-688cc80650c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236858 #edb/ref 1080 "1\\,\\text{km/h}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236858 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236858 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5cca184-297c-5a51-8000-69ff9d31794e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237037 #edb/ref 1080 "0.9\\,\\text{km/h}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237037 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237037 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bfbede8f-435b-56bc-843f-241505817845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237304 #edb/ref 1080 "1.1\\,\\text{km/h}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237304 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237304 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ec10295d-25b1-5a19-b89f-69f7478d51cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186235944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186235977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237729 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237729 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237729 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186237037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237729 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e41e2628-f143-5669-8c9e-14ea2edbfb3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238053 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238053 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238053 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238053 #edb/ref 1071 "James and Mary have a date at the cinema theater.\nJames arrived at the agreed time, but Mary is late. After $20$ minutes of waiting, James decides to go home.\n\nJames travels north from the theater with a speed of $3.2\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Mary travels west toward the theater. When James is $0.8\\,\\text{km}$ from the theater, Mary is $1.5\\,\\text{km}$ from the theater. If the distance between them increases at a speed of $2.3\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is Mary's speed?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238053 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2b855eb-fff2-5496-9e40-b074bb54ef82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238053 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/17/1728c9ca91ef28b19c28048f5a640d0eebbd08f504bb594bc978277fe5620d70.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Mary and the cinema theater,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between James and the cinema theater,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between James and Mary.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=1.5\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=0.8\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {1.5}^{2}+{0.8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2.89 \\\\ z & =1.7\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3.2\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=2.3\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(1.5)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(0.8)(3.2) & =2(1.7)(2.3) \\\\ 3\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =7.82-5.12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mary is approaching the cinema theater with a speed of $0.9\\,\\text{km/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318231 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318231 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318231 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c9725c7b-c6e7-5a47-ac17-323beb7e67b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318324 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318324 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318324 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e3e3fbe8-493b-50a9-863c-e723e85729db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318466 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318466 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318466 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318466 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186318231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318466 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "144a2bd0-8d45-5119-a2f3-32a3e3b10ec9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318736 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318736 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318736 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318736 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186318324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318736 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8d73eb93-a815-542a-870f-ca2e4d920490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319042 #edb/ref 1071 "Alex and Brooke agree to meet at the library. Alex arrives on time, but Brooke is late. After waiting for a while, Alex decides to leave and calls Brooke. They agree to head home instead.\n\nAlex travels north from the library at a speed of $6\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Brooke travels east away from the library. When Alex is $4\\,\\text{km}$ from the library, Brooke is $3\\,\\text{km}$ from the library. If the distance between them is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is Brooke’s speed?\n\nBrooke’s speed is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{ }\\text{km/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16cd8a89-b31e-5ada-a39c-46883ef43ef8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319042 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/46/46263b1e25bc520881d590ccab503ce0d6cd33e91288e81f1d678cde9a641214.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Brooke and the library,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Alex and the library,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Alex and Brooke.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=4\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{4}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 9+16 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=6\\,\\text{km/h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{km/h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(4)(6) & =2(5)(6) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+48 & =60 \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Brooke's speed is $2\\,\\text{km/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319055 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319055 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319055 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319055 #edb/ref 1071 "A surveyor stands $15\\text{ ft}$ away from the base of a radio tower. An elevator inside the tower moves straight up at a constant speed of $20\\text{ ft/s}.$ How fast is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator changing when the elevator is $20\\text{ ft}$ above the ground?\n\nThe distance between the surveyor and the elevator is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "064c7dea-229d-541a-b55e-0089485e7b3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319055 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/7a/7a6f5b05bc0bd89d01b1b521fa35f4bc613e532a06ce04fea26323e6c5dbbafa.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the elevator and the ground,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=15\\,\\text{ft}$ and $y=20\\,\\text{ft}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {15}^{2}+{20}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 625 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =25\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=20\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(15)(0)+2(20)(20) & =2(25)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 800 & =50\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the surveyor and the elevator is $16\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326984 #edb/ref 1080 "48\\,\\text{ mph}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326984 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326984 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0517a6e8-234e-51c5-b2bd-e0d1e53c77e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327009 #edb/ref 1080 "144\\,\\text{ mph}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327009 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327009 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0959f405-eb85-5237-b107-d3116cc23200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327502 #edb/ref 1080 "96\\,\\text{ mph}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327502 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327502 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4063a90a-1d64-57dc-be5e-07071a4b4ae5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327691 #edb/ref 1080 "24\\,\\text{ mph}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327691 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327691 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5722aef2-837c-561c-9a7f-90a69e603283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328818 #edb/ref 1080 "72\\,\\text{ mph}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328818 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328818 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d70c2ccb-5e11-5756-952d-657013f2c226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329501 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186326984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329501 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329501 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329501 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329501 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329501 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329501 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329501 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186326984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329501 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b5087753-f0cc-5f36-bbe7-2c20e2166ba9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329943 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329943 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329943 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329943 #edb/ref 1071 "Two buses, $A$ and $B,$ drive toward a school. Bus $A$ travels south at a constant velocity of $30\\,\\text{ mph},$ while the bus $B$ travels west at a constant velocity of $40\\,\\text{mph}.$ If bus $A$ is $16$ miles from the school, and bus $B$ is $12$ miles from the school, how fast is the distance between the buses decreasing?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329943 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8393e9fb-6ea7-5c8e-84d5-5a0e41ad548b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329943 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a diagram of the situation.\n\n![](images/07/07eb0e89a4dc85160deb45ee13e6189d32bbc905bc638cb1aee891f0504ca950.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between bus $A$ and school,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between bus $B$ and school,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two buses.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=12\\,\\text{mi}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{mi}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =(12)^{2}+(16)^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =400 \\\\ z & =20\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-40\\,\\text{mph},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\,\\text{mph}.$\n\nNote that the rates of change are both negative because the distance to the school is decreasing for both buses. \n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(-40)+2(16)(-30) & =2(20)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 40\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-960-960 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1920}{40} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the buses is decreasing at a rate of $48\\,\\text{mph}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520774 #edb/ref 1080 "5\\text{ ft/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520774 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520774 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "072e352e-d8d1-59a7-b841-bd241577a866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520830 #edb/ref 1080 "5\\,\\text{mph}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520830 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520830 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2041b6e3-2c7b-5767-bee7-c0917fa37089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520852 #edb/ref 1080 "4\\text{ ft/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520852 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520852 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2900f8e2-5f65-5ee6-9082-48e9d9465465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520858 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{5}\\text{ m/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520858 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520858 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2c37f289-4fa3-5f30-a819-93edd8808892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520866 #edb/ref 1080 "5.7\\text{ ft/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520866 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520866 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31136b91-ada4-5499-9ab7-4a99b8df1106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520892 #edb/ref 