 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521415 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521415 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521415 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521415 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b19ef90c-29be-517a-8b07-95053303a6ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521459 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521459 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49014" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521459 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521459 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/35/3556e6b8bda17ab8242b8cd7924e1f5ad975efcbc6c5235667d786b2f0908e1d.png)\n\nTrain $A$ travels east from station $S$ at a constant velocity of $153\\,\\text{km/h},$ and train $B$ travels south toward $S.$ The distance between the trains is increasing at a rate of $35\\text{ km/h}.$  What is the speed of train $B$ when train $A$ is $51\\,\\text{km}$ from the station and train $B$ is $68\\,\\text{km}$ from the station?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521459 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fa1eef6-69ad-599c-9e20-68839bce41b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521459 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/81/81d16bb54bdecca628d769cd08d02dd327c5483851c2421fb595446c34f8a124.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the station and train $A,$\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the station and train $B,$\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two trains.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=51\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=68\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {51}^{2}+{68}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =7\\,225 \\\\ z & =85\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=153\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=35\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(51)(153)+2(68)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(85)(35) \\\\ 136\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,950-15\\,606 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{9\\,656}{136} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-71.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of train $B$ is $71\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521462 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521462 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521462 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521462 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/77/7741295c73895ec39933adec100c9f3fc7e803fa8ea2a02dae543477c45cbea4.png)\n\nA boat $B$ is being pulled in toward a dock $D$ with a rope by a fisherman $F$ standing on the dock. His hands are $4\\text{ ft}$ above the ground, and he is pulling the rope at a rate of $3\\text{ ft/s}$, how fast is the distance between the dock and the boat decreasing when the boat is $3\\text{ft}$ from the dock?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521462 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15178b94-38d6-597d-bc8c-39fd354b56c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521462 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/af/afbe02b63ca4d45ae4d1759d7fa452b57a8b43f073d08b1368f51472e2e4ea8b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the dock and the boat,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the fisherman's hands and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the fisherman's hands and the boat mooring.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{ft}$ and $y=4\\,\\text{ft }.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =9+16 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the fisherman's hands and the ground remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(4)(0) & =2(5)(-3) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-30 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the dock and the boat is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521474 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521474 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521474 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521474 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9d/9dbee02f0c07041dd8424faf683952ada2f7f1a604f9bf89a65588eaf3b6f9f6.png)\n\nThe legs of an isosceles right triangle are increasing. The length, measured in feet, of each leg is given by $x(t)=t+{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. What is the rate of the change of the length of the hypotenuse after $2$ seconds? Give your answer rounded to $1$ decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521474 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2bda6d09-1af6-5a1c-86e9-4f34148c24fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521474 #edb/ref 1074 "In the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the length of the equal sides, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $x,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the length of the equal sides after $2$ seconds. We get \n\n$$\nx=x(2)=(2)+(2)^{2}=6\\text{ ft}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{6}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 72 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & ≈8.485\\,3\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rate of change of the sides is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(t)=2t+1.$ So, when $t=2$ their velocity is\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(2)=2(2)+1=5\\text{ ft/s}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(6)(5)+2(6)(5) & ≈2(8.485\\,3)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & ≈7.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hypotenuse's length is changing at a rate of $7.1\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104077" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521481 #edb/ref 1071 "After attending school, Elizabeth and Jessica leave at the same time. Elizabeth heads north at a speed of $4.5\\text{ mph},$ while Jessica heads east at a speed of $6\\text{ mph}.$ How fast are the two moving away from each other after walking for $2$ hours?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "385d74bf-db85-520e-88d9-f555fc3d02fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521481 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/2b/2b012a853cf973cc68df35bbd94a498fd1d0d907548c1f6d8ca3f842c967c877.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Jessica and the school,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Elizabeth and the school, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Elizabeth and Jessica.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine Elizabeth's distance from school and Jessica's distance from school after $2$ hours. Since their velocities are constants, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot t=4.5(2)=9\\text{ mi}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot t=6(2)=12\\text{ mi}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {9}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 225 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =15\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{mph}$ after $2$ hours,\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6\\,\\text{mph}$ after $2$ hours.\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(9)(4.5)+2(12)(6) & =2(15)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 225 & =30\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between Elizabeth and Jessica increases at a rate of $7.5\\,\\text{mph}$ after walking $2$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521490 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521490 #edb/ref 1071 "The length of one leg of a right triangle increases at the rate of $3\\text{ m/s},$ while the length of the second leg is decreasing at the rate of $2\\text{ m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the hypotenuse changing when the length of the increasing leg is $12\\text{ m}$ and the length of the decreasing leg is $16\\text{ m}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44d55ece-0880-515f-a521-353e6c740d6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521490 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/ee/eee3de464da57671142e2677974b44c63dc2397215ffdd2aa36e2c3d401a647d.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the length of the increasing side,\n\n- $y$ is the length of the decreasing side,\n\n- $z$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {12}^{2}+{16}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 400 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =20\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$  when $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(3)+2(16)(-2) & =2(20)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 8 & =40\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{8}{40} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the hypotenuse is changing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
